::: {.doc-subject}

Find a dominating set for the graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L88689-L88799){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.dominating_set(start_with=None)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Dominating set](../assets/graphs/dominating-set.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="A seven-node graph: pendant 1, triangle 2, 3, 4, a bridge to triangle 5, 6, 7. Nodes 1, 3, and 5 form the dominating set (accent); an accent edge links each other node to a set member that dominates it. The card shows the result [1, 3, 5], size 3." width="100%"}

A dominating set `D` of a graph `G = (V, E)` is a subset of V such that every vertex not in
`D` is adjacent to at least one member of `D`. This method uses a greedy algorithm that
finds some dominating set, but not necessarily the minimum one.
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[start_with]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>

:   Optional node ID to start the algorithm from. If `None`, the smallest node ID is used.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   List of node IDs forming a dominating set.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the `start_with=` node doesn't exist in the graph.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

A simple greedy sweep in $O(V + E)$ that starts from `start_with=` (or the smallest
node ID if unset), repeatedly adds the smallest-ID unmarked node, and marks it and its
neighbors as dominated until every node is covered. The result is deterministic for a
given graph. The result is *some* dominating set but not
the smallest one possible; the minimum dominating set problem is $\mathsf{NP}$-hard,
so this heuristic mainly earns its keep as a fast baseline. Any maximal independent
set is automatically a dominating set, since every excluded vertex must be adjacent
to an included one, so `maximal_independent_set()` provides another cheap route to
the same guarantee. For a weighted objective, `minimum_weighted_dominating_set()` is
preferable.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~minimum_weighted_dominating_set`) 

:   Find a smaller or cheaper dominating set.

[](`~is_dominating_set`) 

:   Check a set of nodes.

[](`~maximal_independent_set`) 

:   A dominating set that is also independent.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

On a path, the sweep starts from `A`, which covers `B`, and then needs `C` and `E` for
the rest, though `B` and `D` would do:

::: {#74ba2e31 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D"), ("D", "E")]
)

[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in graph.dominating_set()]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
['A', 'C', 'E']
```
:::
:::


The sweep's three on the left, and the two that would do on the right. Either way, every
node is picked out or next to one that is:

::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#db5c294e .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
spots = {"A": (0, 0), "B": (1, 1), "C": (2, 0), "D": (3, 1), "E": (4, 0)}
graph.set_node_attrs(
    "pos", {graph.get_id_from_label(v): p for v, p in spots.items()}
)

graph.plot(
    layout="pos", highlight=graph.dominating_set(), label_by="label",
    width=320, height=240, legend=False,
)
```

